hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in Chapter five  of the categories when Aristotle is  discussing substance or Lucia in Greek  which is one of the most important of  the categories there's a preliminary  discussion about substances and what we  call contraries a non TI this is a  important linguistic and metaphysical  term for Aristotle's understanding of  things and how they're related to each  other so Aristotle is trying to work  with with this notion of opposition or  contrariness to each other and you  notice some of the examples of things  that are counter at white black diseased  healthy warm cold bad good a also  included sweet and bitter which is  something that plays an important role  it has discussions of flavors and tastes  and things like that now this is an  issue that had puzzled  the Greek philosophers quite a bit how  could these these opposites or these  contraries be related to each other was  it in terms of substances or things  themselves was  in terms of something else so Aristotle  is working with this in providing us  with an explanation here so there's  three main things that he says in this  account that are particularly important  the third one is the most important but  let's go through all of them so the  first thing that he says is substances  themselves do not have contraries or  opposites now this is an interesting  thing to say because if we think about  you know how we represent things to  ourselves you know we say so-and-so is  the opposite of this person but what we  mean there is going to turn out that  they have some opposite qualities or  even that there's some other thing going  on there it's not as substances  themselves whether as primary that is  individual substances or a secondary  that is species or genus as these  classes of things are associated with  each other it's not as substances per se  that they are opposed to each other  it's rather substances understood as  being in a relation to each other  substances with a certain quality to  each other so substances as substances  do not have contraries Aristotle says  look it doesn't make any sense to talk  about something being the contrary of  human being how could first substances  have them this man for example that  animal nothing is contrary to them now  you know we do have interesting schemes  where we might say well you know dogs  are the opposite of cats right so we're  talking about two different species  there but they're not really the  opposites that's something that we're  adding to it there's nothing in the  scheme of things that makes dogs and  cats opposites to each other  in the way that say black and white or  cold and warm are opposed to each other  Aristotle also says that substances do  not admit of degrees or more literally a  a greater and a lesser so what does he  mean by that he means that insofar as  we're considering human being I am a  human being I am NOT  or human being then say you are or any  other human being we're all equally  human being we're at this is what it  means to be a human being now the  qualities of things do admit of a  greater and a lesser so we can say for  example one dog is a meaner than another  dog and one dog is sweeter than another  dog right or this person is smarter and  this person is Dumber or this person we  got to be a little bit careful relative  terms like greater and lesser like  taller and shorter but those work as  well so it's not just qualities or  quantities we can also talk in terms of  relation and the quality is the fact  that the qualities admit of a more and a  less might fool us into thinking  Aristotle thinks that substances  themselves have a more or less but they  they don't instead what happens is a  substance admits more or less of that  quality so if we talk about a person  being a better person than somebody else  that person has more goodness as the  quality and they could lose that  goodness and become worse right or they  could get more of it and become better  later on Aristotle will use an example  of Justice we can say that this person  is more just than that person just as  per se doesn't admit of a greater or  less or just as simply as what it is but  the person being just predicating just  of them because that's a quality that  they have that can be greater or lesser  what else substances he says remain a  unity and the same but they accept in  the Greek term for that is it's actually  Dec Taccone own that is they are  accepting they are you know taking in  they accept conscious  now what does that mean well if we use  this this you know sort of schema here  the substance itself has certain  qualities  it also has being a substance of that  sort so you know for example if we use  Aristotle's definition of human being or  account to human being rational animal  assuming that we're an adult right  rationality takes a while to develop  there's a whole story there that's part  of my substance those are those are you  know what it is to be a human being  animal rational there's a lot of things  that go along with that but then there's  also other qualities and so a substance  you know can have coloration white black  those are contrary to each other given  what we know about color these days we  can say that certain colors are also  falling into the scheme of contries so  you know we might be able to say blue  and orange or yellow and purple or red  and green are likewise opposed to each  other the way that white and black are  white and black is the example that  Aristotle uses himself and notice that  not every thing can admit these  qualities that are opposed to each other  a given whiteness or a of color cannot  become black and remain whiteness any  given color is what it is the the  existing coloration in something like  for example this book right this book is  green and you notice it's kind of a drab  --is-- green that has a bit of gray I  suppose in it or maybe some Brown Holly  I'm not an artist right or a person who  knows a lot about color unfortunately we  could change the coloration of this book  you know perhaps we put lemon juice on  it or something like that maybe that  changes it or we dip it in tannin and it  becomes browner we're changing the  coloration then it's not the original  coloration that is  you know receiving a new quality is  rather the substance of this this this  cloth that is receiving the quality and  is changing so the the qualities the  contrary qualities can come and go the  substance remains the same and admits of  one or the other this process of  accepting a contrary quality and thereby  losing the other quality is what we call  change Aristotle says so you know he  says we have you know changes within the  the quality of changes within the  substance itself substance admits of  contraries by having received or  accepted them into itself so if  something can go from being white to  being black can go from being healthy to  being diseased right go from being warm  to being cold happens to my coffee all  the time go from being good to being bad  or or vice versa go from being sweet to  being bitter the substance itself is the  thing that is changing the qualities  themselves are not changing there you  might say coming and going out of  existence in that thing in that  substance this is this is something that  Aristotle says is really unique about  substances he says what is most  characteristic or you know specific to  edie own of substances appears to be  this though it remains notwithstanding  numerically one and the same it is  capable of being the recipient of  contrary qualities of things that are  other than substance we could hardly  come up with an example that has this  characteristic and he uses some examples  you know a particular color numerically  one of the same can in no way be both  black and white an action if one in the  same can in no way be both good event  now of course this has to be qualified a  bit it can't be both good and bad in the  same way  the same time it could be good in one  respect and bad in another respect so we  could say well it admits these opposite  qualities but we're we're being you know  we're qualifying it in that case we're  saying in this way it admits this  quality in this way it admits this  quality but if we consider it as a whole  it's not admitting two different  qualities but we as substances and other  substances do in fact do that so this is  a very important feature of substances  it's like Aristotle says it's it's  really you know characteristic of  substances as opposed to other things  that they admit of opposite or contrary  qualities in ways that other things  don't
 

